# Binary Size & Build Modes

Choose from different build configurations to balance features against binary size.

{% grid cols=2 %}
{% card title="Full Build" icon="package" %}
Includes all features: REPL and REST API.
```bash
cargo build --release
```
{% /card %}

{% card title="Slim Build" icon="cube" %}
Minimal binary containing only core tunneling logic.
```bash
cargo build --release --no-default-features
```
{% /card %}
{% /grid %}

{% separator /%}

# mTLS Security

Mutual TLS (mTLS) authenticates and secures tunnel connections. Both endpoints verify each other's identity using X.509 certificates to establish a cryptographically secure tunnel.

### Key Security Features

- **Mutual Authentication:** Both client and server authenticate each other, preventing unauthorized access.
- **End-to-End Encryption:** All traffic is encrypted using TLS 1.3 to ensure confidentiality and integrity.
- **Certificate Validation:** Strict validation ensures only authorized nodes can join the infrastructure.
- **Perfect Forward Secrecy:** Ephemeral session keys prevent retroactive decryption of intercepted traffic.

### Security Benefits

- Prevents man-in-the-middle attacks on tunnel connections.
- Protects sensitive traffic from eavesdropping.
- Complies with zero-trust security models.

### Certificate Generation

Generate a self-signed CA certificate:

```bash
openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 -days 365 -nodes \
  -keyout ca-key.pem -out ca-cert.pem \
  -subj "/CN=wallhack-ca"
```

Generate node certificates signed by the CA:

```bash
openssl req -newkey rsa:4096 -nodes \
  -keyout node-key.pem -out node-req.pem \
  -subj "/CN=wallhack-node"

openssl x509 -req -in node-req.pem -days 365 \
  -CA ca-cert.pem -CAkey ca-key.pem -CAcreateserial \
  -out node-cert.pem
```

### Using mTLS

Entry node requiring client certificates:

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443 --cert node-cert.pem --key node-key.pem --ca ca-cert.pem" title="Entry node with mTLS" /%}

Exit node presenting a client certificate:

{% codeblock code="wallhack exit --connect ENTRY:443 --cert node-cert.pem --key node-key.pem --ca ca-cert.pem" title="Exit node with mTLS" /%}

{% callout type="note" %}
**Best Practice:** Use a unique certificate for each node and rotate them regularly. Store private keys securely.
{% /callout %}

{% separator /%}

# REST API

An optional REST API provides headless management for entry nodes. Enable the API with the `--api` flag. It uses HTTPS and shares the same TLS certificate as the tunnel.

### Capabilities

- **Remote Management:** Control entry nodes via HTTPS to add routes, view peers, or disconnect nodes.
- **Real-time Events:** Subscribe to Server-Sent Events (SSE) for live updates on connections and latency.
- **Authentication:** Protect endpoints with optional basic authentication.
- **Feature-Gated:** Excluded from slim builds to minimize binary size.

### Starting the API

Enable the REST API on an entry node:

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443 --api 127.0.0.1:6566" title="Entry node with API" /%}

Enable the API with authentication:

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443 --api 127.0.0.1:6566 --api-user admin --api-pass secret" title="Authenticated API" /%}

### API Endpoints

| Method | Endpoint | Description |
|--------|----------|-------------|
| GET | `/health` | Health check (no auth required) |
| GET | `/ping` | Version, uptime, and node role |
| GET | `/stats` | Traffic statistics and metrics |
| GET | `/peers` | List connected peers with latency |
| DELETE | `/peers/{id}` | Disconnect a peer |
| POST | `/routes` | Add a route (body: `{"cidr": "10.0.0.0/8"}`) |
| DELETE | `/routes/{cidr}` | Remove a route |
| GET | `/events` | SSE stream for real-time events |

### Example Usage

```bash
# Check health
curl -k https://localhost:6566/health

# Get stats (with auth)
curl -k -u admin:secret https://localhost:6566/stats

# Add a route
curl -k -u admin:secret -X POST https://localhost:6566/routes \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"cidr": "10.0.0.0/8"}'
```

{% callout type="note" %}
The REST API requires the `api` feature: `cargo build --release --features api`.
{% /callout %}

{% separator /%}

# REPL Commands

Interactive sessions provide a command-line interface for real-time control and monitoring.

### Available Commands

| Command | Aliases | Description |
|---------|---------|-------------|
| `ping` | `p` | Show version and uptime |
| `stats` | `s` | Display traffic statistics |
| `peers` | - | List connected peers |
| `sessions` | `t`, `tuns` | List active exit node sessions |
| `route add <cidr>` | - | Add a network route |
| `route del <cidr>` | `route rm` | Remove a network route |
| `disconnect <id>` | `kick`, `kill` | Disconnect a peer |
| `help` | `?` | Show available commands |
| `quit` | `q`, `exit` | Exit the application |

### Example Session

```
wallhack> stats
Traffic Statistics:
  Bytes In:     1.23 MB
  Bytes Out:    456.78 KB

wallhack> route add 10.0.0.0/8
Route added: 10.0.0.0/8
```

{% callout type="note" %}
The REPL is disabled when stdin is not a terminal. Use the REST API for headless management.
{% /callout %}
